welcome to the newshour grain exports  from ukraine are expected to begin in  the next 24 hours 25 million tonnes are  waiting in ukrainian ports where they've  been stuck since russia invaded and  that's caused food shortages and price  rises around the world let's get more of  this from john hendren who's in the city  the port city in fact of odessa so  basically um john if the grain does  leave ukraine it's going to leave from  really where you are  absolutely this is one of three ports  where those grain shipments will be  leaving from and they are where that  grain is being stored right now this is  odessa the first shipments are expected  to go from chernomorsk and then there  would be shipments from pivdeni as well  now there's been an inspection uh site  where uh presumably the russian  officials who can control  some of those areas will will be  inspecting uh those ships but there's  also been a corridor mapped out south  down the coast on the black sea  that is theoretically a safe corridor  but we've seen the the port of odessa  strike uh struck a couple of times i  shouldn't say the port the region of  odessa has been struck a couple of times  and that leaves  some of the farmers who are shipping  this uh grain uncomfortable we talked to  one yesterday in northern ukraine who  said that they were going to store their  grain on site in containers at their  farm because they just don't feel it  safe yet so those first shipments will  really be a test as to whether other  farmers are going to follow suit let's  say john you know areas around odessa  have been targeted it's made the  international community and those  involved in the shipping of grain very  nervous but again there are other areas  of ukraine that have been under  bombardment over the past 24 hours so  bring us up to speed on where we should  be focused here  that's right and i should have mentioned  that 20 million tons of grain are now  sitting at those three ports but you're  right today was a special day here in  ukraine because it was the first day  ukraine was going to celebrate statehood  day and  probably as a link to that  26 rockets were fired from belarus  territory that is of course um in  coordination with russia that's a  russian attack and it struck in northern  ukraine in  we have chicago  learned that there was a power plant  struck in full harris which is in the  dumbass region um and then we have  recently just in the last couple of  hours learned a casualty count from yet  another attack this is one in central  ukraine a rocket attack at krepovnitsky  uh it's in central ukraine and we are  told that five people were killed 25  wounded that's the only one of those  rocket attacks in which we have a  casualty count at this time  john hendren there for us in odessa  thanks very much for the update john  we'll dip in with you through the day  thank you  now a joint coordination center to  monitor grain exports from ukraine  opened in istanbul on wednesday it's  part of that deal where kiev and moscow  signed last week which was brokered by  turkey and the u.n now turkey's defense  minister says it's an example of  international cooperation needed to  tackle global food shortages  ibrahim khalid is spokesman and special  adviser to the turkish president and he  joins me now from istanbul mr clean good  good to have you with us on the program  and thanks very much for your time  one gets the impression that the  situation is still very delicate until  those first ships leave ukraine with the  grain  how concerned is the turkish president  about the situation right now  of course the station remains fragile  critical on all sides the war still is  going on but we've been able to broker  this deal in the middle of this war and  we we have concentrated our efforts in  fact on  some kind of a ceasefire agreement  presumably exchange and eventually a  peace agreement  his negotiation process but it's too  early for that of course to happen at  this point but uh at least we have this  agreement whereby both the ukrainian and  russian agricultural products grain  exports will be reaching international  markets  the process has been rather difficult it  took us months of discrete negotiations  from behind the scenes  talks and  meetings to reach this deal and i think  it will remain also rather critical  sensitive as we go along uh with this  process uh but we have urged all sides  both sides that is both the russian and  ukrainian sides to stick to uh the main  principles of the agreement that they  signed last friday here in istanbul uh  in the presence of our president  president erdogan and the secretary  general and uh with uh that unfortunate  incident that happened a day after on  saturday uh we are we are hoping and  that we are reaching out also to both  sides uh that uh you know things will uh  will move on uh smoothly from this point  onward because it's really in the mutual  interest of both countries uh to have  this operation uh flowing and again  there was a great deal of concern that  as you say less than 24 hours after that  agreement was signed on friday we saw  parts of odessa surrounding areas  targeted uh apparently by russian forces  how much of the president or how much  does the president think that his own  reputation is on the line because when  you underwrite an international  agreement sitting alongside the un  secretary general for something as  delicate as this you really have put  your your reputation uh in the spotlight  and you want both sides to understand  that as well  president erdogan used his own personal  leadership diplomacy to uh make this  deal happen and as i said it took you  know months of negotiations from behind  the scenes and uh in fact if you look at  the  the process  of what happened what unfolded uh over  the last five six months since this war  started this is actually the first major  agreement uh that will deliver something  on the ground not only for 